P5 — ETS big-binary functional ceiling                  beamr lane #62
======================================================================
TREE      e16811597c3c1bde75f0e94c204d0497be8a7e05  (see TREE)
DATE      2026-08-01
BRIEF     BRIEF-62-p4-p5-probes.md
          sha256 d15ac15dd90b86497ec475a740f160557e4982bf939b95c3a7fca75a6b6253ce
PROGRAM   probes/lane-62/src/p5.rs  (committed in-tree, this branch)
RUN       command.txt ; output p5.log ; rc p5.exit (0) ; build rc build.exit (0)
DISK      disk-boundary.log


WHAT WAS MEASURED
-----------------
`ets:insert/2` then `ets:lookup/2` of a row `{1, Bin}` at 64 KiB / 512 KiB /
1 MiB / 4 MiB, on a process created with the default heap
(`DEFAULT_HEAP_SIZE` = 233 words, so `max_capacity` = `DEFAULT_MAX_HEAP_WORDS`
= 131,072 words). Table is `ets:new(p5_probe_table, [])` — set, protected,
keypos 1. Fresh atom table, ETS registry and process per trial. No scheduler
is involved; the BIFs are driven directly through `ProcessContext`. No wall
clock, no RNG.

Then a bisection on byte size, to replace the arithmetic estimate with an
observed boundary.


SIZE TABLE
----------
label    bytes      ets_owned_words  lookup_reserve_words  insert  lookup
64KiB      65536              8197                  8199      OK      OK
512KiB    524288             65541                 65543      OK      OK
1MiB     1048576            131077                131079      OK      badarg
4MiB     4194304            524293                524295      OK      badarg

`ets_owned_words` is measured, not computed: the probe runs the same
`copy_term_to_ets` that `ets:insert` runs and reads `OwnedTerm::total_words()`.
`lookup_reserve_words` adds `list_heap_words(1)` = 2 for the one-element result
list, matching `copy_rows_list` (`native/ets_bifs.rs:1288-1298`).


CROSSOVER (bisection on bytes)
------------------------------
bracket             512 KiB lookup OK ; 1 MiB lookup FAILS
largest OK          971,088 bytes   (948.33 KiB ; 0.926102 MiB) reserve 121,393 words
smallest FAIL       971,089 bytes                               reserve 121,394 words


EXACT ERROR TERM (verbatim, as the caller receives it)
------------------------------------------------------
    Term=Term(73) Atom=Atom(9) name="badarg"

Identical at 1 MiB and 4 MiB. `Atom(9)` is `Atom::BADARG` (`atom/table.rs:23`,
interned as "badarg" at `atom/table.rs:111`).

Independent confirmation of the mechanism, with ETS removed entirely: calling
`ProcessContext::ensure_heap_space` directly for the same word budget on a
fresh default-heap process returns the SAME term.

    64KiB   direct ensure_heap_space(8199)   -> OK
    512KiB  direct ensure_heap_space(65543)  -> OK
    1MiB    direct ensure_heap_space(131079) -> ERR Term=Term(73) Atom=Atom(9) name="badarg"
    4MiB    direct ensure_heap_space(524295) -> ERR Term=Term(73) Atom=Atom(9) name="badarg"


WHAT THE NUMBERS SAY
--------------------
1. IT IS A FUNCTIONAL CEILING, NOT A MEMORY COST. At 1 MiB and above the
   lookup cannot succeed on a default-heap process, at any price. The caller
   receives `badarg` — structurally indistinguishable from a genuinely bad
   argument, because `ensure_heap_space` (`native/context/mod.rs:788-795`)
   maps EVERY `gc::ensure_space` error to `Term::atom(BADARG)` through
   `.map_err(|_| …)`. Confirmed at the bytes; that flattening is the mechanism.

2. THE WRITE PATH AND THE READ PATH DISAGREE. `ets:insert` SUCCEEDED at all
   four sizes, including 4 MiB. The row goes into the table and can never come
   back out to a default-heap process. The asymmetry is structural: insert
   copies into ETS-owned `Box<[u64]>` allocations (`ets/copy.rs:243-252`),
   which are not bounded by any process heap; lookup copies back onto the
   caller heap, which is.

3. A PROCBIN DOES NOT STAY REFCOUNTED ONCE IT IS IN ETS. The argument binary is
   allocated as a 3-word ProcBin referencing off-heap bytes, which is why
   insert is cheap at any size. But `ets/copy.rs:151-154` routes `ProcBin`
   through `copy_binary`, which materialises the bytes INLINE
   (`2 + packed_word_count(len)` words, `ets/copy.rs:234-241`), and the return
   trip does the same (`copy_binary_to_heap`, `ets/copy.rs:369-377`). Sharing
   is lost at the table boundary in both directions.

4. THE BOUNDARY IS NOT WHERE THE CONSTANT PREDICTS — IT IS LOWER. The
   arithmetic in the brief (131,072 words) implies a ceiling near 1 MiB. The
   observed ceiling is 121,393 words / 971,088 bytes, about 7.4% below it.
   131,072 nursery words are UNREACHABLE.

   Mechanism, read at e168115. The nursery grows on a Fibonacci-like ladder:
   `Heap::new` seeds `previous_capacity = fibonacci_previous(233) = 144`
   (`process/heap.rs:265-266`, `:689-698`), and `next_capacity`
   (`process/heap.rs:508-513`) returns `current + previous`. From 233 the
   ladder is

       233, 377, 610, 987, 1597, 2584, 4181, 6765, 10946, 17711,
       28657, 46368, 75025, 121393, 196418, ...

   `grow_to_next_capacity_with_max` (`process/heap.rs:499-506`) REFUSES a step
   whose target exceeds `max_capacity` — it returns `HeapFull`, it does not
   clamp to `max_capacity`. So the largest nursery a default-heap process can
   ever reach is the largest ladder entry <= 131,072, namely 121,393; the next
   rung, 196,418, is refused forever.

   The two instruments agree exactly. Solving the reserve formula at that cap,
   `7 + ceil(B/8) <= 121,393` gives `B <= 971,088` — the bisected boundary to
   the byte, and the bisected reserve is 121,393 words on the nose.

5. Practical restatement: on a default-heap process the ETS big-binary ceiling
   is ~948 KiB of binary payload per looked-up row, not ~1 MiB, and it is a
   hard functional wall reported as `badarg`.


JUDGEMENT CALL
--------------
The brief's caveat says the boundary should be checked "including whether the
boundary sits where the constant predicts". It does not. Item 4 is reported as
a correction to the brief's arithmetic, derived from code read in context and
corroborated by an independent bisection.


